body:
So this callstack didn't make any sense to me at all. The build containers didn't work for me but I finally managed to reproduce the crash by building with mingw gcc 13 on Ubuntu using: `scons platform=windows arch=x86_32 debug_symbols=yes target=template_release use_mingw=yes` and then it started to make sense:

`JoltArea3D::_add_to_space` will create a new rigid body using `jolt_settings`, an object allocated by the constructor of `JoltShapedObject3D`:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/modules/jolt_physics/objects/jolt_shaped_object_3d.h#L54

Using MSVC this `new` call will be routed through:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/thirdparty/jolt_physics/Jolt/Core/Memory.h#L36

Because the class contains `Vec3`'s which must be aligned to 16 bytes.

Compiling using MinGW and adding this above the `new`:

```
static_assert(alignof(JPH::BodyCreationSettings) == 16);
```

I can see that MinGW correctly sees that the class must be 16 byte aligned. It selects a different `new` function though:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/thirdparty/jolt_physics/Jolt/Core/Memory.h#L32

And therefore MinGW goes through:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/modules/jolt_physics/jolt_globals.cpp#L47-L49

With MSVC it goes through:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/modules/jolt_physics/jolt_globals.cpp#L59-L61

The `Memory::alloc_static` function returns an 8-byte aligned block which causes an unaligned read access violation when trying to AND `BodyCreationSettings::mLinearVelocity` to mask out locked axis.

An ugly hack that fixes the issue is to replace:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/1696ab0cb6ff3c77fa48985fa943c81be34a751a/modules/jolt_physics/jolt_globals.cpp#L47-L57

with:

```C++
void *jolt_alloc(size_t p_size) {
	p_size += 16;
	void *data = Memory::alloc_aligned_static(p_size, 16);
	*(size_t *)data = p_size;
	return ((uint8_t *)data + 16);
}

void *jolt_realloc(void *p_mem, size_t p_old_size, size_t p_new_size) {
	if (p_mem == nullptr) {
		return jolt_alloc(p_new_size);
	}

	void *data = (uint8_t *)p_mem - 16;
	size_t old_size = *(size_t *)data;
	p_new_size += 16;
	data = Memory::realloc_aligned_static(data, p_new_size, old_size, 16);
	*(size_t *)data = p_new_size;
	return ((uint8_t *)data + 16);
}

void jolt_free(void *p_mem) {
	if (p_mem != nullptr) {
		void *data = (uint8_t *)p_mem - 16;
		Memory::free_aligned_static(data);
	}
}
```

to force 16 byte alignment. The real problem is with how gcc handles the `new` overload though (I'm going to do some experiments with this).

B.t.w. I see that the godot x86_32 build is still emitting very old school [x87 floating point instructions](https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/X86_Assembly/Floating_Point) instructions whereas Jolt currently requires SSE2.

> B.t.w. I see that the godot x86_32 build is still emitting very old school [x87 floating point instructions](https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/X86_Assembly/Floating_Point) instructions whereas Jolt currently requires SSE2.

`SConstruct` has:
```
if env["arch"] == "x86_32":
    if env.msvc:
        env.Append(CCFLAGS=["/arch:SSE2"])
    else:
        env.Append(CCFLAGS=["-msse2"])
```
so it should be using SSE2. Is that not sufficient?

I've created a test app to check out the behavior of overloading `new` on various platforms:

```C++
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <new>
#include <cstdint>

template <int alignment>
class alignas(alignment) Test
{
public:
	void *operator new (size_t s)
	{ 
		printf("non aligned alloc\n");
		return (void*)1; // returning nullptr doesn't compile and we're not using the return value anyway
	}

	void operator delete (void* ptr) noexcept
	{ 
		printf("non aligned free\n");
	}

	void *operator new (size_t s, std::align_val_t a)
	{ 
		printf("aligned alloc with alignment %d\n", (int)a);
		return (void*)1; 
	}

	void operator delete (void* ptr, std::align_val_t a) noexcept	
	{ 
		printf("aligned free with alignment %d\n", (int)a);
	}
};

int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
	printf("sizeof(void*): %d\n", (int)sizeof(void*));

	printf("alignof(max_align_t): %d\n", (int)alignof(max_align_t));

	constexpr int max_align = 32;
	int al = max_align;
	for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i)
	{
		void* ptr = malloc(i);
		int new_al = int(uintptr_t(ptr) % max_align);
		if (new_al > 0 && new_al < al)
			al = new_al;
		// leak so we get a different address next time
	}
	printf("measured malloc alignment: %d\n", al);

	printf("\n8 byte aligned\n");
	Test<8>* t8 = new Test<8>();
	delete t8;

	printf("\n16 byte aligned\n");
	Test<16>* t16 = new Test<16>();
	delete t16;

	printf("\n32 byte aligned\n");
	Test<32>* t32 = new Test<32>();
	delete t32;

	return 0;
}
```

When compiling with MinGW using MSYS2 (`i686-w64-mingw32-g++ test.cpp`) the program returns:

```
sizeof(void*): 4
alignof(max_align_t): 8
measured malloc alignment: 8

8 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

16 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

32 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 32
aligned free with alignment 32
```

This shows that a class that is 16 byte aligned will use the normal new overload and since malloc returns a block aligned to 8 we can get alignment exceptions.

On Ubuntu with GCC (`g++ -m32 test.cpp`):

```
sizeof(void*): 4
alignof(max_align_t): 16
measured malloc alignment: 16

8 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

16 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

32 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 32
aligned free with alignment 32
```

We see that it also calls the non-aligned allocator but malloc returns 16 byte aligned blocks, so we're fine.

Ubuntu with clang (`clang++ -m32 test.cpp`):

```
sizeof(void*): 4
alignof(max_align_t): 8
measured malloc alignment: 16

8 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

16 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 16
aligned free with alignment 16

32 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 32
aligned free with alignment 32
```

Again no issue here, even though `max_align_t` is 8 byte aligned, malloc returns 16 byte aligned blocks.

MSVC in the x86 commandline environment (`cl /std:c++17 test.cpp`):

```
sizeof(void*): 4
alignof(max_align_t): 8
measured malloc alignment: 8

8 byte aligned
non aligned alloc
non aligned free

16 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 16
aligned free with alignment 16

32 byte aligned
aligned alloc with alignment 32
aligned free with alignment 32
```

Correctly calls the aligned alloc for 16 byte aligned classes.

64-bit builds all work fine too as `malloc` by default returns 16 byte aligned blocks.

I've created a workaround for this issue and will be submitting a PR shortly.
